// Grade 10 passages (validated by live-patches/grade-journey/validate.mjs). Built by validate.mjs --write.
export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g10-u1-p01","unit":"G10-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Stop Changing the Clocks","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Twice a year, most Americans perform a strange ritual. In March they lose an hour of sleep, and in November they get it back. The custom feels as permanent as the seasons, but it is a policy choice, and it is one we should finally abandon. The United States should stop switching its clocks and pick a single time to keep all year.\n\nStart with the history, because it undercuts the most common defense. Daylight saving time was not invented for farmers. Many farmers actually opposed it, since their cows did not adjust their schedules to match the clock. The idea spread during the First World War, when governments hoped that extra evening light would save fuel. The modern American system dates to the Uniform Time Act of 1966, which set rules for when the switch would happen but allowed states to opt out. Hawaii and most of Arizona already stay on standard time all year, and their residents seem to manage perfectly well.\n\nThe strongest reason to stop is health. Losing an hour each spring is a small shock to the body, but it lands on millions of people at once. Sleep researchers have linked the spring change to a short rise in heart attacks and to more traffic crashes in the days that follow. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine has called for ending the seasonal switch. These effects are modest for any one person, yet spread across an entire country they add up to real harm that serves no clear purpose. Doctors also note that the effects of the lost hour can linger for a week or more, especially for teenagers, whose internal clocks already run late.\n\nThe promised energy savings, meanwhile, have mostly faded. Lighting is now a small share of what homes use, and in hot regions an extra hour of evening sun can mean more air conditioning, not less. When Indiana adopted daylight saving time statewide in 2006, one study of household electricity found that use went up slightly rather than down. A policy justified by saving energy should at least save energy.\n\nCritics raise a fair objection. If we stop changing the clocks, which time should we keep? Many lawmakers prefer permanent daylight saving time, which would give brighter evenings for sports, shopping, and outdoor recreation. In 2022 the Senate passed a bill to do exactly that, though it never became law. Sleep scientists, however, warn that permanent daylight saving time would leave many northern cities in darkness until nearly nine in the morning in winter, sending teenagers to school long before sunrise. The country tried a year-round version in 1974, and it became so unpopular that Congress reversed it within months.\n\nThis disagreement is real, but it is not a reason to keep switching. It is a reason to choose carefully. Standard time matches our clocks more closely to the sun, which is why most sleep experts favor it. Even a nation that chose the other option would at least spare itself the twice-yearly jolt. Either choice is better than continuing a ritual that most people dislike and few can explain.\n\nSome people simply enjoy the long summer evenings and fear losing them. That feeling deserves respect. But nothing stops businesses, schools, and sports leagues from shifting their own hours in summer, the way many already set different schedules for different seasons. Individuals and institutions can adapt their routines gradually; it is far harder for a body to adapt to a sudden change imposed on everyone at once.\n\nThe clock is a tool, and tools should serve the people who use them. For decades we have rearranged our lives around a change that offers little benefit and some measurable cost. It is time to set the clocks one last time and leave them alone. We do not need another study to tell us that the twice-yearly scramble of reset ovens, missed meetings, and groggy commutes is a nuisance we could simply choose to end.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What is the author's main claim?","options":["Daylight time should be extended into winter","Each state should invent its own clock rules","The country should keep one time all year"],"answer":2},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author respond to people who love long summer evenings?","options":["Summer evenings are unhealthy for teenagers","Groups can shift their own hours in summer","Long evenings waste more energy than they save"],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"According to the passage, what did a study find after Indiana adopted the change in 2006?","options":["Household electricity use went up slightly","Electricity use fell by a large amount","Most families saved money on lighting"],"answer":0,"evidence":"When Indiana adopted daylight saving time statewide in 2006, one study of household electricity found that use went up slightly rather than down."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the author mention the 1974 experiment?","options":["To prove the Senate bill finally became law","To explain why farmers liked daylight time","To show permanent daylight time failed before"],"answer":2}],"grade":10,"words":665,"auto":{"fk":8.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u1-p02","unit":"G10-U1","pool":"practice","title":"Free the Curb: End Parking Minimums","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Drive through almost any American suburb and you will notice that the biggest feature of the landscape is not the stores, the restaurants or the apartment buildings. It is the asphalt around them. Much of that pavement is there not because business owners wanted it, but because the law required it. For decades, city zoning codes have set \"parking minimums,\" rules that say how many spaces a new building must provide. A restaurant might need one space for every three seats; a dentist's office, five spaces for every thousand square feet; an apartment, two spaces for every unit. Cities should abolish these requirements and let builders decide how much parking their customers actually need.\n\nThe first problem with parking minimums is that many of the numbers are close to guesswork. Planners often copied figures from other towns or from engineering tables based on surveys of busy suburban sites on their busiest days. The urban planning scholar Donald Shoup, whose 2005 book The High Cost of Free Parking made the case against minimums famous, argued that these rules rest on weak evidence and are rarely tested against reality. A requirement designed for the day after Thanksgiving at a shopping mall makes little sense for a corner bakery next to a bus stop.\n\nThe second problem is cost. Parking is never free; someone pays for it. A single space in a surface lot uses land that could hold housing or shops, and a space in an underground garage can cost tens of thousands of dollars to build. When a city requires those spaces, the price is folded into rents, mortgages and the cost of every meal and haircut, whether or not a customer owns a car. People who walk, bike or ride transit, often people with lower incomes, end up paying for parking they never use.\n\nThird, minimums quietly block the kind of housing many cities say they want. On a small lot, the space needed for required parking can make a modest apartment building impossible to fit or impossible to finance. Older neighborhoods full of corner stores and small apartment buildings, the places people now pay a premium to live in, would often be illegal to build today because they lack the required parking.\n\nCities that have dropped the rules offer evidence that the sky does not fall. Buffalo, New York, removed its citywide parking minimums in 2017, and Minneapolis followed in 2021. In 2023, Austin, Texas, became the largest American city at that time to eliminate them entirely. Studies of Buffalo found that developers still built parking, but they built it where it made sense, and many projects included fewer spaces than the old code would have demanded.\n\nOpponents raise a fair concern. If new buildings provide less parking, they say, drivers will crowd onto neighborhood streets, and residents who depend on cars will circle the block for spaces that are no longer there. That worry deserves an answer rather than a dismissal. But the answer is better management, not mandatory asphalt. Cities can price street parking so that a few spaces stay open on every block, offer residential permits, and use the money to improve sidewalks and transit. Ending minimums does not ban parking; a grocery store that needs a large lot can still build one.\n\nThe deeper issue is who gets to decide. Today, a planning code written decades ago tells a builder exactly how much of a property must be set aside for cars, regardless of location, customers or cost. Removing that rule lets the people who know a site best make the choice, and lets the rest of us stop paying for empty spaces. A city is meant to be a place for people. It is time our laws stopped treating storage for cars as its most important use.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What is the author's central claim?","options":["Cities should let builders decide how much parking to provide","Cities should ban new parking lots in every neighborhood","Cities should build more garages to free up their streets"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that parking rules raise costs for people without cars?","options":["Planners often copied figures from other towns or from engineering tables based on surveys of busy suburban sites on their busiest days.","People who walk, bike or ride transit, often people with lower incomes, end up paying for parking they never use.","Buffalo, New York, removed its citywide parking minimums in 2017, and Minneapolis followed in 2021."],"answer":1,"evidence":"People who walk, bike or ride transit, often people with lower incomes, end up paying for parking they never use."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author respond to the worry about crowded neighborhood streets?","options":["By arguing that drivers should simply give up their cars","By proposing priced street parking and resident permits","By admitting that minimums should stay in busy areas"],"answer":1},{"type":"vocab","q":"In paragraph 4, what does 'premium' mean?","options":["a discount offered to new residents","a reward given for good behavior","an extra amount above the usual price"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Older neighborhoods full of corner stores and small apartment buildings, the places people now pay a premium to live in, would often be illegal to build today because they lack the required parking."}],"grade":10,"words":633,"auto":{"fk":9.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u1-c01","unit":"G10-U1","pool":"check","title":"It Is Time to Retire the Tip","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"At the end of a restaurant meal in the United States, diners face a small test of arithmetic and conscience. Fifteen percent? Twenty? More, because the server was friendly, or less, because the kitchen was slow? Increasingly the question follows us beyond restaurants, onto tablet screens at coffee counters, bakeries and even self-checkout kiosks. Americans have come to treat tipping as a fixed part of life. It is not. It is a policy choice, and a poor one. Restaurants should pay their workers a full wage and set prices that reflect it, and states should end the lower \"tipped minimum wage\" that makes this system possible.\n\nBegin with how the law works. Under federal law, employers may pay tipped workers as little as two dollars and thirteen cents an hour, a figure that has not changed since 1991, as long as tips bring the worker up to the regular federal minimum. In practice, this means that a large share of a server's income depends not on the employer but on the mood, generosity and assumptions of strangers. Pay can swing widely from a busy Friday to a rainy Tuesday, which makes it hard for workers to plan rent, childcare or savings.\n\nSecond, tipping is a poor measure of good work. Researchers who study restaurant behavior have found that tip size is only weakly connected to the quality of service a customer reports. Tips are shaped by factors that have little to do with skill: the size of the bill, the weather, whether the diner is in a hurry, and, troublingly, the server's appearance. Studies have found that customers' biases about race, age and attractiveness can show up in what they leave on the table. A pay system that rewards workers partly for things they cannot control is not a fair one.\n\nThird, dependence on tips can put workers in uncomfortable positions. Servers who rely on customers for most of their income may feel pressure to tolerate rude or inappropriate behavior rather than risk a smaller tip. Advocates for restaurant workers have argued that a stable wage would give employees more freedom to set boundaries and more security to report problems.\n\nThere is evidence that a different system can work. Seven states, including California, Washington, Oregon and Minnesota, require employers to pay tipped workers the full state minimum wage before tips. Restaurants in those states continue to open and operate, and diners there still eat out. Many other countries, such as Japan, treat service as part of the price and do not expect customers to tip at all.\n\nDefenders of tipping make a serious point. Some servers at busy restaurants earn more under the current system than a fixed wage would pay, and many of them like it that way. Owners warn that higher labor costs would force them to raise menu prices or cut staff, and restaurants already operate on thin margins. These concerns are real, but they argue for a careful transition, not for keeping a broken system. Menu prices would rise, but the total a diner pays need not, because the tip would already be included. Restaurants could also share service charges openly with kitchen staff, who often earn less than servers and are usually left out of tips altogether.\n\nTipping persists mostly because it is familiar. But familiarity is not a justification. A worker's ability to pay rent should not depend on whether a stranger had a good day. If a meal costs what it costs, the menu should say so, and the people who cook and serve it should be paid by the business that profits from their work.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"Which statement best expresses the author's main claim?","options":["Restaurants should pay full wages and build service into prices","Diners should always leave at least twenty percent for servers","Restaurants should replace their servers with tablets and kiosks"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that tips fail to reward good work reliably?","options":["Pay can swing widely from a busy Friday to a rainy Tuesday, which makes it hard for workers to plan rent, childcare or savings.","Tips are shaped by factors that have little to do with skill: the size of the bill, the weather, whether the diner is in a hurry, and, troublingly, the server's appearance.","Restaurants could also share service charges openly with kitchen staff, who often earn less than servers and are usually left out of tips altogether."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Tips are shaped by factors that have little to do with skill: the size of the bill, the weather, whether the diner is in a hurry, and, troublingly, the server's appearance."},{"type":"claim","q":"Which counterclaim does the author address in paragraph 6?","options":["Tipped workers in most states already earn high wages","Some servers earn more from tips than a wage would pay","Diners in Japan leave larger tips than American diners"],"answer":1},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author mention states such as California and Oregon?","options":["To show that most restaurants there have recently closed","To show that tipping is growing fastest on the West Coast","To show that full wages for servers already work in practice"],"answer":2},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the final paragraph, what does 'justification' mean?","options":["a good reason for something","a legal penalty for something","a common habit among diners"],"answer":0,"evidence":"But familiarity is not a justification."}],"grade":10,"words":604,"auto":{"fk":8.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u1-c02","unit":"G10-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Lawn Has Had Its Day","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The American lawn is a strange kind of garden. It produces no food, offers little shelter to wildlife, and demands constant attention: mowing, watering, fertilizing, and weeding. Yet it covers an enormous area of the country. Researchers using satellite data have estimated that turf grass covers more land in the United States than any single irrigated crop. It is time to stop treating the green lawn as a default and start treating it as a choice, one that in many places we can no longer afford.\n\nWater is the most urgent reason. According to the Environmental Protection Agency, roughly thirty percent of the water an average American household uses goes to outdoor purposes, and much of that is sprayed onto grass. In dry regions the share is far higher. The Colorado River, which supplies water to some forty million people, has been shrinking under years of drought and heavy demand. Pouring that water onto lawns that no one walks on is hard to defend. Even in wetter states, summer watering can strain local supplies just when rivers and reservoirs are lowest.\n\nSome places have already acted. In 2021 Nevada passed a law requiring the removal of decorative grass, the kind that sits in road medians and office parks, in the Las Vegas area by 2027. The local water authority had already paid residents for years to replace their lawns with desert plants. These programs show that a community can change its landscape without giving up beauty. A yard planted with native shrubs, grasses, and wildflowers can be striking, and it often attracts birds and pollinators that a lawn cannot support. Native plants are also adapted to local rainfall, so once they are established they usually need far less water than grass.\n\nLawns also carry hidden costs beyond water. Gas-powered mowers and leaf blowers produce air pollution and noise, and their small, simple engines are often far dirtier than the engines in modern cars, which have to meet much stricter rules. Fertilizers that wash off lawns can feed algae blooms in rivers and lakes. Every weekend, millions of hours go into maintaining a surface whose main purpose is to look like every other surface on the street.\n\nDefenders of the lawn make a fair point: grass is useful. Children play on it, dogs run across it, and families gather on it. A soccer field cannot be replaced with cactus. But this objection supports a smaller lawn, not no lawn at all. The argument here is against grass that serves no one, the strip beside the driveway or the corner of the yard that is mowed but never used. Keep the patch where the kids actually play, and let the rest become something else.\n\nOthers worry about rules. Many homeowners associations require residents to keep a neat green lawn, and some have fined people for letting their yards turn brown during droughts. These rules reflect habits, not laws of nature, and habits can change. Several states, including Colorado and Texas, have passed laws limiting how strictly associations can forbid water-saving landscapes. As more neighbors make the switch, the patchwork of native gardens will begin to look normal rather than neglected. Some cities now offer rebates to homeowners who replace grass with plants that need less water.\n\nThe lawn became the standard American yard partly because it signaled that a family had the time and money to maintain something purely decorative. Before the twentieth century, closely trimmed lawns were found mostly on the estates of the wealthy, who could pay workers to cut them by hand. That message made sense in a wetter, emptier century. Today, in a country facing drought, rising temperatures, and declining insect populations, the most responsible yard may be the one that asks for less. A lawn is not a moral failing. But a lawn that exists only out of habit is a luxury, and luxuries should be the first things we give up when resources run short.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"Which statement best expresses the author's claim?","options":["Every lawn in the country should be outlawed","Lawns are the main cause of western droughts","Lawns that serve no purpose should be replaced"],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that lawns use up scarce water?","options":["Gas-powered mowers and leaf blowers produce air pollution and noise, and their small, simple engines are often far dirtier than the engines in modern cars, which have to meet much stricter rules.","According to the Environmental Protection Agency, roughly thirty percent of the water an average American household uses goes to outdoor purposes, and much of that is sprayed onto grass.","These rules reflect habits, not laws of nature, and habits can change."],"answer":1,"evidence":"According to the Environmental Protection Agency, roughly thirty percent of the water an average American household uses goes to outdoor purposes, and much of that is sprayed onto grass."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author respond to the point that children and dogs use lawns?","options":["It argues for smaller lawns, not none","Children should play in public parks","Dogs harm lawns more than droughts do"],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the author mention that Nevada's water authority paid residents?","options":["To prove that Nevada has no lawns left","To argue that desert plants cost too much","To show lawn replacement works in practice"],"answer":2},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does 'signaled' most nearly mean?","options":["kept secret","made known","paid for"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The lawn became the standard American yard partly because it signaled that a family had the time and money to maintain something purely decorative."}],"grade":10,"words":661,"auto":{"fk":8.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u1-c03","unit":"G10-U1","pool":"check","title":"Teach the Money Class","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"By the time they finish high school, most American students can identify the parts of a cell, balance a chemical equation and explain the causes of the Civil War. Far fewer can explain how interest on a credit card is calculated, read a pay stub, or compare two student loan offers. Yet within months of graduation, many of them will sign a lease, open a credit card, take a first full-time job or borrow thousands of dollars for college. Every state should require a semester-long personal finance course for high school graduation.\n\nThe strongest reason is that the decisions young adults face are expensive and hard to reverse. A missed credit card payment can damage a credit score for years, making it more costly to rent an apartment or finance a car. Student loans, unlike most other debts, are very difficult to erase through bankruptcy. A course that explains compound interest, credit reports and the true cost of borrowing gives students the tools to avoid mistakes before they make them rather than after.\n\nSecond, a requirement makes access fair. Some teenagers learn about money at home, from parents who discuss budgets, retirement accounts and taxes at the dinner table. Many others do not, often because their own families never had the chance to learn. When personal finance is an elective, or left to chance, the students who most need the knowledge are the least likely to get it. A graduation requirement ensures that every student receives the same basic preparation, regardless of zip code or family background.\n\nThird, there is growing evidence that well-designed courses make a difference. Economists have studied states that added personal finance requirements and compared students who graduated before and after the change. Several of these studies found that, once the requirement took effect, young people in those states had better credit scores and lower rates of delinquent debt. They were also more likely to choose lower-cost ways to pay for college, such as federal loans instead of private loans or credit cards. The effects were not enormous, but they were measurable.\n\nThe idea is also gaining ground. As recently as 2020, fewer than ten states guaranteed every student a personal finance course. By 2024, roughly half of the states had passed laws requiring one, often with support from both political parties. These states have shown that the change is practical: schools can train teachers, adopt free curricula from nonprofit groups and fit the course into a crowded schedule.\n\nCritics raise reasonable objections. Some argue that the school day is already full and that adding a requirement means squeezing out art, music or another elective. Others point to older research suggesting that a single class does little to change adult behavior, especially when it is taught by teachers with no training in the subject. These criticisms are worth taking seriously. But they are arguments for doing the course well, not for skipping it. The studies showing poor results often examined short units buried inside other classes; the more promising results come from dedicated, semester-long courses taught by trained teachers. As for the crowded schedule, schools already make choices about what every graduate must know. Knowing how to avoid a predatory loan belongs on that list.\n\nNo class can guarantee wise choices, and money troubles often come from low wages and emergencies rather than ignorance. Financial education is not a substitute for fair pay or a strong safety net. But a young person who understands interest, budgets and contracts will face those challenges with clearer eyes. Schools prepare students for college and careers. They should also prepare them for the first bill.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"Which statement best expresses the author's claim?","options":["Parents should teach their children about money at home instead","Every state should make a money course a graduation requirement","Schools should drop electives in order to teach more economics"],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that finance courses can change how young adults behave?","options":["These states have shown that the change is practical: schools can train teachers, adopt free curricula from nonprofit groups and fit the course into a crowded schedule.","Several of these studies found that, once the requirement took effect, young people in those states had better credit scores and lower rates of delinquent debt.","Some teenagers learn about money at home, from parents who discuss budgets, retirement accounts and taxes at the dinner table."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Several of these studies found that, once the requirement took effect, young people in those states had better credit scores and lower rates of delinquent debt."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author answer critics who cite older research?","options":["Critics have rarely visited a modern classroom","Older research was paid for by banks and is biased","Weak results came from short units, not full courses"],"answer":2},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author admit in the last paragraph that no class can guarantee wise choices?","options":["To acknowledge limits while still keeping the claim","To abandon the claim in favor of raising wages","To suggest that the course should stay optional"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Based on the author's reasoning in paragraph 3, who would gain most from a requirement?","options":["Students who already plan to study economics","Students whose families never learned about money","Teachers who hope to offer more art electives"],"answer":1}],"grade":10,"words":610,"auto":{"fk":9.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u3-p01","unit":"G10-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Mendeleev's Gaps","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In the winter of 1869, a Russian chemistry professor named Dmitri Mendeleev was struggling with a practical problem. He was writing a textbook, and he needed a sensible order in which to present the roughly sixty elements then known to science. Chemists already knew a great deal about individual elements: how heavy their atoms were compared with hydrogen, how they reacted, which ones formed similar compounds. What they lacked was a single pattern that tied all of this information together. Mendeleev's solution would become one of the most famous diagrams in science, the periodic table, and its real power lay not only in what it showed but in what it left out.\n\nMendeleev was not the first to notice that elements fell into families. Earlier chemists had grouped elements in threes with similar properties, and in the 1860s the English chemist John Newlands observed that when elements were listed by atomic weight, similar properties seemed to recur every eighth element. Newlands compared this pattern to the octaves of a musical scale, and some of his colleagues mocked the idea. The German chemist Lothar Meyer was developing a similar table at nearly the same time as Mendeleev. The idea of a periodic pattern, in other words, was in the air.\n\nWhat set Mendeleev apart was his willingness to trust the pattern more than the data. He arranged the elements mostly in order of increasing atomic weight, placing elements with similar chemical behavior in the same column. When an element did not fit, he did not simply force it into the next open position. Sometimes he concluded that its atomic weight had been measured incorrectly. In a few places he swapped the order of two elements because their chemistry clearly placed them in each other's columns. Most boldly, he left blank spaces in the table where he believed elements existed that no one had yet discovered.\n\nMendeleev did not merely leave these gaps empty. He predicted the properties of the missing elements in detail, using the trends of their neighbors above, below and beside them. For one element, which he called eka-aluminium, he predicted an atomic weight of about 68, a low melting point and a particular density. In 1875 a French chemist, Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran, discovered a new metal in zinc ore and named it gallium. Its properties matched Mendeleev's forecast closely; when Mendeleev pointed out that the first measured density seemed wrong, a more careful measurement brought it in line with his prediction. Scandium, discovered in 1879, and germanium, discovered in 1886, filled two more of his gaps, and both again resembled his descriptions.\n\nThese successful predictions transformed the periodic table from a convenient filing system into a scientific theory. A theory is valuable not only because it organizes what we already know, but because it makes claims that could turn out to be wrong. Mendeleev had taken that risk publicly, and the discoveries proved him right.\n\nMendeleev's table was not perfect. He did not predict the noble gases, such as argon and neon, which were discovered in the 1890s and required an entire new column. He also could not explain why the pattern existed. The answer came in the early twentieth century, when the English physicist Henry Moseley showed that each element has a characteristic atomic number, the number of protons in its nucleus. Ordering elements by atomic number rather than atomic weight resolved the swaps that had puzzled Mendeleev, and later work on the arrangement of electrons explained why elements in the same column behave alike.\n\nToday the table hangs in nearly every chemistry classroom, extended to more than a hundred elements, including one named mendelevium in his honor. Its lasting lesson is about how science advances. Mendeleev worked with incomplete and sometimes faulty data, yet by looking for the deeper pattern, and daring to predict what had not been seen, he showed that nature is more orderly than it first appears.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central idea of the passage?","options":["Mendeleev's table was abandoned once atomic numbers were discovered","Mendeleev was the first scientist to sort the elements into families","Mendeleev's table won acceptance because its predictions came true"],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"Which element filled the gap Mendeleev called eka-aluminium?","options":["scandium","gallium","germanium"],"answer":1,"evidence":"In 1875 a French chemist, Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran, discovered a new metal in zinc ore and named it gallium."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did the successful predictions matter so much to the table's reputation?","options":["They turned a filing system into a testable theory","They proved that atomic weights were always measured correctly","They showed that Newlands had been right all along"],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In paragraph 6, what does 'resolved' mean?","options":["made worse or deeper","settled or cleared up","hid or covered over"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Ordering elements by atomic number rather than atomic weight resolved the swaps that had puzzled Mendeleev, and later work on the arrangement of electrons explained why elements in the same column behave alike."}],"grade":10,"words":657,"auto":{"fk":10.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u3-p02","unit":"G10-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Magna Carta: The Charter That Grew","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In June 1215, in a meadow called Runnymede beside the River Thames, King John of England agreed to a long list of demands from a group of rebellious barons. The document that recorded the agreement came to be known as Magna Carta, Latin for \"Great Charter.\" Today it is celebrated as a cornerstone of liberty and the rule of law. Yet the charter's original purpose was far narrower, and its fame is largely the work of the centuries that followed it. The story of Magna Carta is the story of how a failed peace treaty became a powerful idea.\n\nKing John was not a popular ruler. He had lost valuable lands in France, and to pay for wars to recover them he demanded heavy payments from his barons, the wealthy landholders who owed him loyalty. Many barons felt he was abusing customs that had limited earlier kings. His reputation suffered further from rumors about the fate of his young nephew Arthur, a rival claimant to the throne who had vanished in royal custody. In 1215 a group of them took up arms, captured London, and forced the king to negotiate.\n\nThe resulting charter dealt mostly with the concerns of these powerful men. Many of its clauses addressed feudal payments, inheritance, the rights of widows, fishing weirs on rivers, and the king's control of forests. Ordinary farm workers, who made up most of England's population, were barely mentioned. Read in full, the charter looks less like a declaration of human rights than like a settlement of a property dispute among the rich.\n\nIt also failed almost immediately. Neither side trusted the other, and within weeks John asked Pope Innocent III to cancel the agreement. The pope declared it void in August 1215, and England fell into civil war. When John died the following year, however, the advisers of his young son, Henry III, reissued a revised version of the charter to win back the barons' support. It was issued again in 1217 and, in a lasting form, in 1225. Through these reissues, the charter became an established part of English law instead of a broken treaty.\n\nA few clauses contained ideas that reached beyond their time. One declared that no free man could be imprisoned, stripped of his property, or exiled except by the lawful judgment of his equals or by the law of the land. Another promised that justice would not be sold, denied, or delayed. The first of these, now usually called clause 39, is still quoted by judges and lawyers around the world. These lines did not create jury trials or modern courts, but they expressed a principle that would prove explosive: even the king was bound by law.\n\nThat principle was revived again and again. In the seventeenth century, English lawyers such as Edward Coke used Magna Carta to challenge kings who claimed unlimited power. American colonists cited it when they protested taxation by a distant Parliament, and its influence can be seen in the guarantee of due process in the United States Constitution. Each generation read into the old charter the freedoms it needed, sometimes stretching its original meaning considerably. In 1957 the American Bar Association built a memorial at Runnymede to honor the charter's influence on American law.\n\nToday only a handful of the charter's clauses remain law in England, and most of the original text is of interest mainly to historians. Four copies of the 1215 version survive, written in Latin on parchment. In 2015 the charter's eight hundredth anniversary was celebrated with exhibitions in both Britain and the United States. Their power lies less in their exact words than in what people have made of them. Magna Carta shows that a document's meaning is not fixed on the day it is sealed. It can grow, sometimes far beyond what its authors intended, when later readers find in it a tool for their own struggles.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","options":["Magna Carta gave ordinary farmers rights for the first time","King John wrote Magna Carta to protect the poor from barons","A narrow, failed deal grew into a lasting symbol of law"],"answer":2},{"type":"sequence","q":"What happened right after the pope declared the charter void?","options":["John sealed a second charter","England fell into civil war","The barons accepted peace"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The pope declared it void in August 1215, and England fell into civil war."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does the author list clauses about fishing weirs and forests?","options":["To show the charter mainly served powerful landowners","To prove that rivers mattered more than human rights","To explain why King John lost his lands in France"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"Based on the last two paragraphs, what does the author suggest about seminal documents?","options":["Their original words must never be reinterpreted","They lose all importance once they are outdated","Their meaning can grow as later readers use them"],"answer":2}],"grade":10,"words":651,"auto":{"fk":9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u3-c01","unit":"G10-U3","pool":"check","title":"Madison's Answer to Faction","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In the fall of 1787, the proposed Constitution of the United States faced a difficult test. It had been written in secret over a long summer in Philadelphia, and now it needed the approval of state conventions. In New York, opponents attacked it in newspapers, warning that a powerful national government would crush the liberties Americans had just fought a revolution to win. Three supporters, Alexander Hamilton, James Madison, and John Jay, answered with a series of eighty-five essays published under a single pen name, \"Publius.\" Today they are known as The Federalist Papers, and the tenth essay, written by Madison, is often considered the most important of them all.\n\nFederalist No. 10 takes on a problem that worried nearly every thinker of the time: faction. By faction, Madison meant any group of citizens, whether a majority or a minority, united by a shared passion or interest that runs against the rights of others or the good of the whole community. Factions might form around religion, around political leaders, or, most commonly, around property. Those who own land and those who do not, those who lend money and those who borrow it, will naturally want different things from their government.\n\nMadison considered two ways of dealing with faction. The first was to remove its causes. A government could destroy liberty, the condition that allows factions to form, but Madison argued that this cure would be far worse than the disease. In one of the essay's most famous lines, he wrote that \"Liberty is to faction what air is to fire.\" Taking away air would put out the fire, but it would also make life impossible. The other way to remove the causes would be to give every citizen the same opinions and interests, which Madison dismissed as impossible. As long as people are free to think and are unequal in talents and fortunes, they will disagree.\n\nIf the causes of faction cannot be removed, Madison reasoned, then a well-designed government must control its effects. A minority faction posed less danger, because in a republic the majority could simply outvote it. The real threat was a majority faction, a large group able to use the machinery of democracy to oppress everyone else. Small direct democracies, like those of ancient Greece, had often collapsed for exactly this reason.\n\nMadison's solution was surprising. Many writers of his era believed that republics could survive only in small territories, where citizens shared similar values. Madison argued the opposite. A large republic, he claimed, would contain so many different interests that it would be hard for any one faction to become a majority. A farmer in Georgia, a merchant in Boston, and a shipbuilder in Philadelphia would rarely want exactly the same thing. To build a majority, groups would have to compromise, and compromise would soften their demands.\n\nHe also argued that representation would help. Instead of voting directly on every law, citizens would choose representatives, who might be wiser and less swept up by temporary passions than the public as a whole. In a large republic, each representative would be chosen by many voters, which Madison hoped would make it harder for unworthy candidates to win through local tricks.\n\nNot every reader has found the argument convincing. Critics have pointed out that Madison's design was also meant to protect the property of the wealthy from demands by poorer citizens, and that the system he praised left out enslaved people, women, and many men without property. Others note that modern technology allows people with similar views to find one another across great distances, weakening the protection that size was supposed to provide.\n\nStill, Federalist No. 10 remains essential reading because it asks a question every democracy must face. How can a government give power to the people while protecting individuals and minorities from the people's worst impulses? Madison's answer was not to eliminate disagreement but to multiply it, trusting that a nation with many competing voices would be harder to capture than a nation with only a few.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central idea of Federalist No. 10, as the passage explains it?","options":["A large republic can control the effects of faction","Factions can be ended by limiting citizens' liberty","Small democracies best protect the rights of minorities"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Madison's view of republics was unusual for his time?","options":["In New York, opponents attacked it in newspapers, warning that a powerful national government would crush the liberties Americans had just fought a revolution to win.","A minority faction posed less danger, because in a republic the majority could simply outvote it.","Many writers of his era believed that republics could survive only in small territories, where citizens shared similar values."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Many writers of his era believed that republics could survive only in small territories, where citizens shared similar values."},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does Madison compare liberty to air and faction to fire?","options":["To argue that factions spread through a nation like fire","To show that removing liberty would destroy life itself","To warn readers that liberty is too dangerous to allow"],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"According to the passage, what did Madison see as the greatest danger?","options":["A majority faction","A minority faction","A foreign army"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The real threat was a majority faction, a large group able to use the machinery of democracy to oppress everyone else."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author include the paragraph about critics?","options":["To prove that Madison's ideas were finally rejected","To explain why Hamilton wrote most of the essays","To show the essay's limits as well as its strengths"],"answer":2}],"grade":10,"words":674,"auto":{"fk":10.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u3-c02","unit":"G10-U3","pool":"check","title":"Seneca Falls and the Long Road to the Vote","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In July 1848, about three hundred people gathered in a chapel in the small town of Seneca Falls, New York. They had come for a two-day meeting announced only days earlier in a local newspaper, which promised a discussion of \"the social, civil, and religious condition and rights of woman.\" Few of them could have guessed that historians would one day call it the beginning of the organized women's rights movement in the United States. The chapel itself still stands, preserved as part of a national historical park.\n\nThe meeting grew out of a friendship. Elizabeth Cady Stanton, a young mother living in Seneca Falls, had met the Quaker activist Lucretia Mott years earlier at an antislavery conference in London. There, women delegates had been refused seats on the main floor because of their sex. The insult stayed with both women. When Mott visited the area in the summer of 1848, Mott was already a well-known speaker, and her presence would help draw a crowd. Stanton and a few friends decided over tea to hold a convention of their own.\n\nThe central document of the convention was the Declaration of Sentiments, drafted largely by Stanton. Its structure was bold and deliberate. It borrowed the form and much of the language of the Declaration of Independence, with one crucial change: \"We hold these truths to be self-evident: that all men and women are created equal.\" Just as the original declaration had listed the abuses of King George III, Stanton's version listed the ways men had denied women their rights. Married women could not control their own property or wages. Women were shut out of most colleges and professions. Above all, they could not vote.\n\nBy echoing the nation's founding document, Stanton made a pointed argument. If Americans truly believed the principles of 1776, then they had to apply them to half the population. The strategy turned a familiar text into a mirror, forcing readers to see the gap between the country's ideals and its laws.\n\nMost of the convention's resolutions passed without much debate. One did not. The ninth resolution declared that it was the duty of women to secure for themselves the right to vote. Even some supporters thought this demand was too radical and would make the whole movement look foolish. Mott herself reportedly worried that it would invite ridicule. The resolution passed narrowly, after a powerful speech in its favor by Frederick Douglass, the formerly enslaved abolitionist and newspaper editor, who argued that political power was essential to every other right.\n\nIn the end, about one hundred of those present signed the declaration, sixty-eight women and thirty-two men. Newspapers mocked the meeting, and some signers, embarrassed by the attention, later asked to have their names removed. Yet the convention inspired others across the country. Women's rights meetings soon followed in other states, and leaders such as Susan B. Anthony, who joined the cause a few years later, built national organizations that pressed the demand for decades.\n\nVictory came slowly. The Nineteenth Amendment, which barred states from denying the vote on account of sex, was ratified in 1920, seventy-two years after Seneca Falls. Of the women who signed the declaration, only one, Charlotte Woodward, is known to have lived long enough to see it become law. Even then, many women of color continued to face barriers at the polls for decades, through poll taxes, literacy tests, and intimidation.\n\nThe Seneca Falls convention matters less for what it achieved immediately than for what it began. It set out a list of demands that would guide reformers for generations, and it showed how the words of a founding document could be turned toward a more complete idea of freedom. Later movements would use the same strategy, holding the nation to the promises it had already made.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"What is the central idea of the passage?","options":["The Seneca Falls meeting won women the right to vote at once","Seneca Falls began a long, influential women's rights campaign","Frederick Douglass organized the first women's rights meeting"],"answer":1},{"type":"author","q":"Why did Stanton model her declaration on the Declaration of Independence?","options":["To expose the gap between national ideals and laws","To show she disagreed with the nation's founders","To avoid writing a new document in her own words"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that voting was the most controversial demand?","options":["Married women could not control their own property or wages.","Newspapers mocked the meeting, and some signers, embarrassed by the attention, later asked to have their names removed.","Even some supporters thought this demand was too radical and would make the whole movement look foolish."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Even some supporters thought this demand was too radical and would make the whole movement look foolish."},{"type":"sequence","q":"What came just before the ninth resolution passed?","options":["A vote held by the newspaper editors","A powerful speech by Frederick Douglass","A walkout led by Lucretia Mott and Stanton"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The resolution passed narrowly, after a powerful speech in its favor by Frederick Douglass, the formerly enslaved abolitionist and newspaper editor, who argued that political power was essential to every other right."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the detail about Charlotte Woodward suggest?","options":["Winning the vote took most of a lifetime","Few women at Seneca Falls cared about voting","Woodward led the fight for the amendment"],"answer":0}],"grade":10,"words":641,"auto":{"fk":9.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u3-c03","unit":"G10-U3","pool":"check","title":"The Fragile Miracle of Antibiotics","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In September 1928, the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned from a vacation to his cluttered laboratory in London and noticed something odd. A mold had landed on one of his dishes of bacteria, and around it the bacteria had failed to grow. Fleming identified a substance made by the mold, which he called penicillin. His observation is often told as a story of lucky accident, but the full history of antibiotics is better understood as a story of collaboration, and of a warning that the world has been slow to heed.\n\nFleming's discovery did not immediately help patients. Penicillin was difficult to purify and unstable, and for about a decade it remained a laboratory curiosity. In the late 1930s a team at the University of Oxford, led by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, took up the problem. Working with limited equipment, and with a biochemist named Norman Heatley who designed clever ways to extract the drug, they showed in 1940 that penicillin could protect infected mice. Early human trials followed. Supplies were so scarce that researchers sometimes recovered the drug from patients' urine to use it again.\n\nMass production required a different kind of effort. During the Second World War, British and American scientists, government agencies, and drug companies worked together to grow the mold in huge tanks. A strain found on a moldy cantaloupe in a market in Peoria, Illinois, turned out to produce far more penicillin than Fleming's original. By the end of the war, penicillin was being made in enormous quantities. Fleming, Florey, and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1945.\n\nThe decades that followed are sometimes called the golden age of antibiotics. Scientists discovered drug after drug, many of them from microbes living in soil. Infections that had once been feared, such as pneumonia and infected wounds, became routine to treat. Surgery, cancer treatment, and organ transplants all depend on the ability to prevent and cure bacterial infections.\n\nYet Fleming himself had warned of a problem. In his Nobel lecture he cautioned that bacteria could become resistant if people took too little of the drug or used it carelessly. Resistance is a matter of evolution. When an antibiotic kills most bacteria in a population, any that happen to survive can multiply and pass on their resistance. Every use of an antibiotic, necessary or not, gives resistant bacteria an advantage.\n\nThat warning has proved accurate. Antibiotics are often prescribed for colds and other viral illnesses, which they cannot treat, and in many countries large amounts are given to farm animals. Meanwhile, the discovery of new kinds of antibiotics has slowed dramatically since the 1980s, partly because developing them is expensive and offers drug companies little profit. A drug meant to be used sparingly, as a last line of defense, is a poor business investment. Health agencies now consider antimicrobial resistance one of the most serious threats to global health, and researchers estimate that it is directly responsible for more than a million deaths each year.\n\nThere are ways forward. Doctors can prescribe antibiotics more carefully, and patients can take them exactly as directed and avoid demanding them for viral infections. Governments are experimenting with new ways to reward companies for developing drugs, and researchers are searching unusual environments, from deep soils to the human body itself, for new compounds. Better hygiene and vaccines reduce the number of infections that need treatment in the first place.\n\nThe history of penicillin shows what people can achieve when they work together on a shared problem. It also shows that a scientific triumph is not permanent. Antibiotics are less like a solved puzzle than like a resource that must be managed, and the choices made now will decide how long their protection lasts.","questions":[{"type":"main","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","options":["Fleming alone turned penicillin into a usable medicine","Antibiotic resistance was solved during the golden age","Antibiotics were a shared triumph now threatened by misuse"],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that penicillin's success depended on many people?","options":["Antibiotics are often prescribed for colds and other viral illnesses, which they cannot treat, and in many countries large amounts are given to farm animals.","During the Second World War, British and American scientists, government agencies, and drug companies worked together to grow the mold in huge tanks.","A mold had landed on one of his dishes of bacteria, and around it the bacteria had failed to grow."],"answer":1,"evidence":"During the Second World War, British and American scientists, government agencies, and drug companies worked together to grow the mold in huge tanks."},{"type":"why","q":"According to the passage, why have few new kinds of antibiotics been developed recently?","options":["Drugs used rarely earn little profit","Soil microbes no longer make new drugs","Governments banned antibiotic research"],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In the sixth paragraph, what does 'sparingly' mean?","options":["often and in bulk","mostly on animals","rarely and with care"],"answer":2,"evidence":"A drug meant to be used sparingly, as a last line of defense, is a poor business investment."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author end by comparing antibiotics to a resource that must be managed?","options":["To suggest antibiotics should earn more profit","To stress that their protection can run out","To explain how antibiotics are made from soil"],"answer":1}],"grade":10,"words":626,"auto":{"fk":10.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u5-p01","unit":"G10-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Lead Lines","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"The window arrived at Aunt Noor's studio in eleven wooden crates, each one labeled in a church secretary's careful handwriting, with notes such as \"Panel 3, upper left\" and \"Panel 9, cracked\" written in pencil on the lids. Inside, wrapped in old moving blankets, lay the pieces of a window that had looked down on the same congregation for a hundred and twelve years, until a winter storm sent a branch through its lower corner.\n\nRania had spent enough summers in the studio to know the rules: gloves for the old lead, goggles for cutting, and no food anywhere near the worktables. But she had never been allowed to touch anything this old, and she approached the crates with a caution that surprised her. When her aunt lifted the first panel onto the light table and switched it on, the glass woke up. Blues deepened into the color of evening; a fold of red cloth glowed as if it had been dipped in fire. Rania forgot, for a moment, that she was supposed to be bored by a summer job her mother had arranged.\n\n\"Most people think the glass is the hard part,\" Aunt Noor said, tracing a dark seam with her gloved finger. \"It isn't, because the real trouble is always the lead, which holds everything together and is also the first thing to fail.\" She explained that the narrow channels of metal, called cames, slowly weaken and sag over decades of summer heat and winter cold. The glass had not changed; the skeleton around it had grown tired.\n\nFor the first week, Rania's job was to make rubbings. She laid thin paper over each panel and pressed a stick of black wax across it until the pattern of lead lines appeared, a map of every seam. \"If we lose the map,\" her aunt said, \"we lose the window.\" Rania thought it was the most tedious work she had ever done, until she began to notice the small irregularities that only a slow eye could catch. In the lamb's panel, one line wandered where no line needed to be, a small detour around a piece of pale green glass. In another, a cracked piece had been repaired once before, decades ago, with a thin strip of lead running right across a saint's sleeve like a scar.\n\n\"Should we replace that one?\" Rania asked, pointing to the old repair.\n\nAunt Noor considered the question for a long time before answering. \"We could, and we would make it look new,\" she said finally. \"But someone saved this window before us. That line is part of its story now.\"\n\nThe broken corner was a different matter. Three pieces of deep blue had shattered beyond saving, and her aunt spent two days at the supplier's racks, holding sheets of glass up to the light, rejecting one after another. None of the modern blues were quite right; they were too even, too clean. The old glass had bubbles trapped inside it and streaks where the color had pooled as it cooled. At last she found a sheet with a faint ripple in it, and she cut the new pieces herself while Rania held her breath.\n\nOn the final day, after the new cames had been soldered and the cement brushed into every seam, they lifted the repaired panel onto the light table together. Rania looked for the place where the storm had broken through. She found it only because she knew where to look: three blues slightly brighter than their neighbors, like the voice of someone who has just joined a song.\n\n\"You can tell,\" she said, almost disappointed.\n\n\"You're supposed to be able to tell, if you look closely,\" her aunt replied. \"We're not trying to pretend that nothing happened to this window; we're trying to make sure it can keep standing in the light.\"\n\nRania thought about the old repair across the saint's sleeve, and about whoever would open these crates in another hundred years. She took a pencil from the table and, in the corner of the newest rubbing, wrote the date and both of their names.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"What does the description of the glass that 'woke up' on the light table suggest?","options":["The glass has been damaged by the heat","Rania is frightened by the bright light","Rania is suddenly drawn into the work"],"answer":2},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the story develop?","options":["Repairs can honor an object's history rather than erase it","Old objects should always be replaced with perfect new ones","Careful work matters far less than speed and low cost"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Aunt Noor values earlier repairs?","options":["None of the modern blues were quite right; they were too even, too clean.","That line is part of its story now.","Rania looked for the place where the storm had broken through."],"answer":1,"evidence":"That line is part of its story now."},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of the simile 'like the voice of someone who has just joined a song'?","options":["It shows that the new glass has ruined the old window","It shows the new glass fitting in while still standing out","It suggests that a choir is singing beside the window"],"answer":1}],"grade":10,"words":686,"auto":{"fk":7.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u5-p02","unit":"G10-U5","pool":"practice","title":"Night Shift, Bakery","genre":"poetry","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: Night Shift, Bakery (a poem)\n\nMy father goes to work when the city\nturns its pockets inside out\nand finds them empty: no buses, no voices,\n\nonly the orange hum of streetlights\nleaning over the road like tired nurses.\nHe unlocks the bakery with a key\n\nworn smooth as a river stone.\nBy two the ovens are awake.\nBy three the dough has started breathing,\n\nrising in its steel bowls\nthe way a sleeper's chest rises,\nslow and certain, asking nothing.\n\nHe shapes the loaves with his palms,\nfolding and turning, folding and turning,\na conversation he has had ten thousand times\n\nand still listens to.\nFlour settles on his eyebrows\nuntil he looks older than he is,\n\na sketch of the man he will become.\nBy five the windows sweat.\nBy six the first customers arrive,\n\nnurses ending their shifts, a boy\nwith a newspaper route, a woman\nwho buys one roll and eats it at the counter\n\nwithout speaking, every morning,\nas if the bread were a letter\nshe has been waiting for.\n\nWhen I wake for school he is coming home,\nsmelling of yeast and burnt sugar,\nhis day ending as mine begins.\n\nWe pass in the hallway like two trains\non parallel tracks. He touches my head.\nThe house fills with the warm, plain smell\n\nof something made while I was sleeping,\nsomething made so that I could.\n\n## Text 2: A Note on the Poem\n\nI wrote this poem during the summer I turned sixteen, when I started waking early enough to see my father leave for work. For years I had known his schedule as a fact, the way I knew the date of his birthday. That summer I began to see it as a life.\n\nThe first choice I made was structure. The poem moves through a single night, and I wanted the reader to feel the hours pass, so I used plain time markers: \"By two,\" \"By three,\" \"By five,\" \"By six.\" They work like the ticking of a clock that no one else in the city is awake to hear. I also broke the poem into stanzas of three lines. Three is an unsettled number; it never feels quite finished, and many of my sentences run from one stanza into the next. I hoped that would mirror the way a night shift never really pauses.\n\nThe second choice was comparison. I wanted the bakery to feel alive, so the dough breathes like a sleeper and the streetlights lean like tired nurses. Those similes are not decoration. The nurses return later as real customers, people who also work while others sleep. My father is not the only person keeping the city running in the dark, and the poem needed to show him as part of that hidden community.\n\nI also repeated one phrase, \"folding and turning,\" because repetition is the heart of his job. He makes the same motions every night, yet he still pays attention to them. I wanted the line break after \"ten thousand times\" to make the reader pause, so that \"and still listens to\" arrives as a small surprise.\n\nFinally, I spent the most time on the last line. In an early draft, the poem ended with the smell of bread filling the house. It was pleasant, but it let the reader off too easily. By adding \"something made so that I could,\" I wanted the line to point two ways at once. Most simply, he works at night so that I can sleep. But I also mean that he works so that I can do other things: go to school, choose a future, have mornings that belong to me. I left the sentence unfinished on purpose, so that readers would complete it for themselves.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"What effect do the repeated time markers, such as 'By two' and 'By three,' create?","options":["They show the father is often late to work","They make the reader feel the night passing","They suggest the bakery is losing customers"],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the poet compare the streetlights to tired nurses?","options":["To link the father to others who work at night","To show that the father once worked in a hospital","To suggest that the street is unsafe after dark"],"answer":0},{"type":"compare","q":"How does the note add to a reader’s understanding of the final line?","options":["It reveals the line was borrowed from another poem","It explains that the father wrote the line himself","It shows the line points to both sleep and a future"],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"According to the note, why did the poet choose three-line stanzas?","options":["Three lines are easy to remember","Three never feels quite finished","Three matches the father's three jobs"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Three is an unsettled number; it never feels quite finished, and many of my sentences run from one stanza into the next."}],"grade":10,"words":628,"auto":{"fk":6.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u5-c01","unit":"G10-U5","pool":"check","title":"Shakespeare: Sonnets of Time and Love","genre":"poetry","source":"William Shakespeare, Sonnets 18, 29, 60, 73, 116 and 130, from Shake-speares Sonnets (London: Thomas Thorpe, 1609), in modern spelling","license":"public-domain","text":"## Sonnet 18\n\nShall I compare thee to a summer's day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer's lease hath all too short a date;\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimmed;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature's changing course untrimmed;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow'st;\nNor shall Death brag thou wander'st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow'st:\nSo long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.\n\n## Sonnet 29\n\nWhen, in disgrace with fortune and men's eyes,\nI all alone beweep my outcast state,\nAnd trouble deaf heaven with my bootless cries,\nAnd look upon myself and curse my fate,\nWishing me like to one more rich in hope,\nFeatured like him, like him with friends possessed,\nDesiring this man's art and that man's scope,\nWith what I most enjoy contented least;\nYet in these thoughts myself almost despising,\nHaply I think on thee, and then my state,\nLike to the lark at break of day arising\nFrom sullen earth, sings hymns at heaven's gate;\nFor thy sweet love remembered such wealth brings\nThat then I scorn to change my state with kings.\n\n## Sonnet 60\n\nLike as the waves make towards the pebbled shore,\nSo do our minutes hasten to their end;\nEach changing place with that which goes before,\nIn sequent toil all forwards do contend.\nNativity, once in the main of light,\nCrawls to maturity, wherewith being crowned,\nCrooked eclipses 'gainst his glory fight,\nAnd Time that gave doth now his gift confound.\nTime doth transfix the flourish set on youth\nAnd delves the parallels in beauty's brow,\nFeeds on the rarities of nature's truth,\nAnd nothing stands but for his scythe to mow:\nAnd yet to times in hope my verse shall stand,\nPraising thy worth, despite his cruel hand.\n\n## Sonnet 73\n\nThat time of year thou mayst in me behold\nWhen yellow leaves, or none, or few, do hang\nUpon those boughs which shake against the cold,\nBare ruined choirs, where late the sweet birds sang.\nIn me thou see'st the twilight of such day\nAs after sunset fadeth in the west,\nWhich by and by black night doth take away,\nDeath's second self, that seals up all in rest.\nIn me thou see'st the glowing of such fire\nThat on the ashes of his youth doth lie,\nAs the death-bed whereon it must expire,\nConsumed with that which it was nourished by.\nThis thou perceiv'st, which makes thy love more strong,\nTo love that well which thou must leave ere long.\n\n## Sonnet 116\n\nLet me not to the marriage of true minds\nAdmit impediments. Love is not love\nWhich alters when it alteration finds,\nOr bends with the remover to remove.\nO no! it is an ever-fixed mark\nThat looks on tempests and is never shaken;\nIt is the star to every wand'ring bark,\nWhose worth's unknown, although his height be taken.\nLove's not Time's fool, though rosy lips and cheeks\nWithin his bending sickle's compass come;\nLove alters not with his brief hours and weeks,\nBut bears it out even to the edge of doom.\nIf this be error and upon me proved,\nI never writ, nor no man ever loved.\n\n## Sonnet 130\n\nMy mistress' eyes are nothing like the sun;\nCoral is far more red than her lips' red;\nIf snow be white, why then her breasts are dun;\nIf hairs be wires, black wires grow on her head.\nI have seen roses damasked, red and white,\nBut no such roses see I in her cheeks;\nAnd in some perfumes is there more delight\nThan in the breath that from my mistress reeks.\nI love to hear her speak, yet well I know\nThat music hath a far more pleasing sound;\nI grant I never saw a goddess go;\nMy mistress, when she walks, treads on the ground.\nAnd yet, by heaven, I think my love as rare\nAs any she belied with false compare.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"In Sonnet 73, what do the images of autumn, twilight and a dying fire have in common?","options":["Each shows the speaker's anger at a friend","Each describes a season of new growth","Each shows something nearing its end"],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that true love stays constant through change?","options":["Love is not love Which alters when it alteration finds, Or bends with the remover to remove.","Like as the waves make towards the pebbled shore, So do our minutes hasten to their end; Each changing place with that which goes before, In sequent toil all forwards do contend.","This thou perceiv'st, which makes thy love more strong, To love that well which thou must leave ere long."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Love is not love Which alters when it alteration finds, Or bends with the remover to remove."},{"type":"craft","q":"How does Sonnet 130 treat the usual comparisons found in love poems?","options":["It mocks inflated praise to express an honest love","It uses them sincerely to praise a real goddess","It rejects love entirely as a foolish illusion"],"answer":0},{"type":"compare","q":"What claim do the couplets of Sonnets 18 and 60 share about poetry?","options":["Verse fades even faster than marble and stone","Verse can preserve a person's worth against time","Verse matters far less than a lover's beauty"],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"In Sonnet 29, what causes the speaker's mood to shift?","options":["Hearing a lark sing at his window","Receiving great riches from a king","Remembering the love of the beloved"],"answer":2}],"grade":10,"words":703,"auto":{"fk":12.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u5-c02","unit":"G10-U5","pool":"check","title":"Two Views of Mile Twenty","genre":"poetry","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: Mile Twenty\n\nThey tell you about the wall\nthe way they tell you about weather\nin a city you have never lived in:\na fact for other people.\n\nThen, somewhere past the bridge,\nthe road tilts up by an inch\nno surveyor could measure,\nand your legs become\ntwo borrowed things\nyou must return in better shape\nthan you received them.\n\nThe crowd thins here.\nA man in a folding chair\nholds a sign that says\nYOU ARE NOT TIRED,\nand you want to argue with him,\nbut arguing takes breath.\n\nWhat is left is small:\nthe next cone, the next corner,\nthe orange slice a child holds out\nlike a coin she found\nand has decided you deserve.\n\nYou take it.\nIt is the sweetest thing\nanyone has ever handed you.\n\nSix miles. You count them\nthe way your grandmother counted stitches,\nnot to reach the end\nbut to stay inside the work.\n\nSomewhere ahead\nthere is a clock, a medal, a blanket\nthat crinkles like foil.\nHere there is only this:\nthe left foot,\nthe right foot,\nthe stubborn grammar\nof going on.\n\n## Text 2: What I Learned at Mile Twenty\n\nI ran my first marathon at nineteen, mostly because my older cousin said I couldn't. For four months I followed a training plan taped to my refrigerator, adding a mile or two to my long run each weekend. By the final month, I could run eighteen miles without stopping, and I was privately convinced that the last eight would take care of themselves.\n\nEvery experienced runner I met warned me about \"the wall,\" the point late in a race when the body seems to run out of fuel. Physiologists explain it in terms of glycogen, the stored form of carbohydrate that muscles burn during long efforts. The body can store only so much, and around the twentieth mile many runners begin to run low. When that happens, the body leans more heavily on fat, which it converts to energy more slowly, and a pace that felt comfortable an hour earlier suddenly feels impossible.\n\nI understood all of this the way you understand a news report about a distant country. Then, just past the twentieth mile marker, it stopped being information and became my life. My legs felt heavy and strangely unfamiliar. My thoughts narrowed until I could hold only one at a time. I remember reading a sign held by a stranger and feeling irrationally offended by its cheerfulness.\n\nWhat surprised me was not the pain, which I had expected, but how little the big goal helped. Thinking about the finish, six miles away, made the distance feel endless. So I stopped thinking about it. I picked a lamppost and ran to it. Then a mailbox. Then a woman in a purple jacket who was walking and running in turns. At one water station, a small girl handed me a slice of orange, and I nearly cried, partly from exhaustion and partly because the gesture was so simple and so kind.\n\nI finished in a time no one would admire, but I finished. Since then I have run several more marathons, and I still hit the wall in most of them; better training and more careful eating have pushed it back but never made it disappear. What has changed is how I treat it. I no longer see it as a sign that something has gone wrong. It is simply the part of the race where the big plan stops working and the small steps begin.\n\nI think of mile twenty now whenever a project or a semester feels too large to finish. The lesson is not that willpower conquers everything. It is that endurance, at its hardest, is made of very small decisions, repeated.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"In the poem, what does comparing the orange slice to 'a coin she found' suggest?","options":["The child's small gift feels precious to the runner","The child is selling oranges to the passing runners","The runner has dropped money somewhere on the course"],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of the poem's closing image, 'the stubborn grammar / of going on'?","options":["It suggests the runner is studying language while racing","It presents persistence as a pattern built step by step","It shows the runner quitting just before reaching the finish"],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from the memoir best supports the idea that small targets helped the writer keep going?","options":["My thoughts narrowed until I could hold only one at a time.","I picked a lamppost and ran to it.","I finished in a time no one would admire, but I finished."],"answer":1,"evidence":"I picked a lamppost and ran to it."},{"type":"compare","q":"How do the two texts differ in their treatment of the wall?","options":["Both texts argue that new runners should avoid marathons entirely","The poem explains its biology; the memoir describes only the crowd","The memoir explains its biology; the poem conveys how it feels"],"answer":2},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme do both texts share?","options":["Endurance is built from small, repeated choices","Careful training removes all pain from racing","Crowds matter more than effort in long races"],"answer":0}],"grade":10,"words":630,"auto":{"fk":6.8,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-u5-c03","unit":"G10-U5","pool":"check","title":"The Last Whistle","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"For forty-one years, the whistle at the Harmon paper mill sounded at six in the morning, at two in the afternoon, and at ten at night. It was not a pretty sound but a long, hoarse bellow that rolled down the valley, rattled the windows on Fulton Street, and set every dog in town barking, and nobody in Harmon owned an alarm clock that mattered more.\n\nMy mother worked the two o'clock shift for nineteen of those years, and I grew up believing the whistle belonged to her, the way some children believe the moon follows their car. When it blew in the afternoon, I imagined her pulling on her gloves and stepping into the roar of the machines, a small figure swallowed by a building the size of a hill. When it blew at ten, I listened for her truck in the driveway, and I could usually fall asleep before the headlights swept across my ceiling.\n\nThe announcement arrived in March, on a sheet of paper taped to the door of the break room: the company had been sold, and the new owners would close the Harmon mill by the end of summer. My mother read it aloud at dinner in a flat voice, as if she were reciting the weather forecast. Then she folded it into a careful square and put it in the drawer with the batteries and the takeout menus, a place for things we might eventually need but did not want to look at.\n\nAll spring the town behaved like a family pretending not to notice a crack spreading across the ceiling. The diner kept serving pie, the high school held its graduation, and people talked hopefully about retraining programs and a warehouse that might open two counties over. But the whistle kept sounding, three times a day, and every time it did, someone in the grocery line went quiet for a second, counting.\n\nThe last shift ended on a Friday in August. Half the town drove out to the mill to wait in the parking lot, although nobody had organized anything, and we stood beside our cars in the heavy evening heat while children chased each other between the trucks. Someone had brought a cooler of lemonade. It felt like a fireworks celebration, except that everyone knew exactly what was coming, and no one wanted it to start.\n\nAt ten o'clock the whistle blew. It was the same ragged bellow it had always been, but this time it went on longer, much longer, as if the operator inside had leaned on the lever and could not bring himself to let go. The sound rolled across the lot and up into the hills and came back to us, thinner, from somewhere far away. Then it stopped, and in the silence I heard crickets, a car door closing, and my mother breathing beside me.\n\nThe workers came out through the gate in a slow, uneven line; some of them were laughing, and some were not. My mother walked straight to our truck, set her gloves on the dashboard, and sat for a long time without turning the key.\n\nThe building is still there. Weeds have pushed through the cracked parking lot, and someone has painted a red heart on the loading dock. My mother works at a hospital now, on a schedule that changes every week, and she sets three alarms on her phone. They chime politely, a bright, interchangeable melody that sounds identical in every house in America.\n\nSometimes, at ten at night, I still catch myself listening, not for a whistle, exactly, but for the space where it used to be, which turns out to be its own particular kind of sound.","questions":[{"type":"craft","q":"What does the drawer with batteries and takeout menus suggest about the closing notice?","options":["The notice means little to the family","The family wants to set bad news aside","The mother plans to sell the batteries"],"answer":1},{"type":"craft","q":"What is the effect of comparing the town to a family ignoring a crack in the ceiling?","options":["It shows people avoiding a problem they know is real","It suggests many homes in town needed new repairs","It reveals that families argued about the mill"],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best shows that the final whistle carried emotional weight for the workers?","options":["When it blew in the afternoon, I imagined her pulling on her gloves and stepping into the roar of the machines, a small figure swallowed by a building the size of a hill.","Someone had brought a cooler of lemonade.","It was the same ragged bellow it had always been, but this time it went on longer, much longer, as if the operator inside had leaned on the lever and could not bring himself to let go."],"answer":2,"evidence":"It was the same ragged bellow it had always been, but this time it went on longer, much longer, as if the operator inside had leaned on the lever and could not bring himself to let go."},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author contrast the whistle with the phone alarms?","options":["To prove that phone alarms are more reliable","To show that a shared town identity has faded","To suggest that the mother dislikes her new job"],"answer":1},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the story develop?","options":["Loss is often felt most in the absence it leaves","Hard work always leads to lasting security","Small towns resist change better than big cities"],"answer":0}],"grade":10,"words":619,"auto":{"fk":9.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-book-ch1","unit":"G10-U2","pool":"book","title":"The Dome on Ridge Road","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Patient Sky","chapter":1,"text":"Tomás Reyes had expected a telescope the size of a school bus. What he found at the end of Ridge Road, twenty minutes outside Silver City, New Mexico, was a white dome no bigger than a garden shed, perched on a concrete pad behind a farmhouse, and a seventy-four-year-old woman in a fleece jacket who did not look up from her laptop when he knocked.\n\n\"You must be the volunteer the school mentioned, the one who is supposedly interested in astronomy,\" said Margit Halvorsen, still typing. \"Call me Margit, by the way; Mrs. Halvorsen was my mother-in-law, and she was terrifying.\"\n\nTomás said that he was interested, which was mostly true. He was interested in astronomy the way he was interested in deep-sea diving, as a collection of spectacular photographs he had scrolled past at midnight. He was also interested in the forty hours of community service he needed before graduation, and the observatory had been the only listing that did not involve picking up trash along the highway.\n\nMargit had taught high school physics in Silver City for thirty-one years, and she still spoke as though a bell might ring at any moment and send her students scattering into the hallway. She showed him the telescope, a squat white tube on a heavy mount, and explained that it moved by itself, guided by software, pointing at one galaxy after another through the night and photographing each. Then she showed him what the photographs looked like, and Tomás tried unsuccessfully to hide his disappointment. They were gray rectangles speckled with white dots, some sharp and some smudged, and none of them looked remotely like the swirling purple nebulae on his phone.\n\n\"Beautiful pictures come from enormous telescopes and a great deal of processing,\" she said, reading his expression accurately, \"and we are not here for beauty, because we are hunting.\"\n\nWhen he asked what they were hunting, she opened two images side by side on the screen. \"Supernovae, which are exploding stars,\" she said. \"This is a galaxy photographed last night, and this is the same galaxy photographed three years ago. If a star in that galaxy has exploded since then, there will be a new point of light in the recent image that was absent from the old one, and our job is to find it before anyone else does.\"\n\nShe showed him how the software could flip rapidly between the two images, so that anything new would appear to blink. Tomás watched the galaxy flicker back and forth for nearly a minute, but nothing blinked.\n\nHe asked how often she actually found one, and she answered without any embarrassment that in twenty-two years of searching she had discovered exactly two. Tomás did the arithmetic and decided not to say it aloud, so she said it for him. \"That is roughly one every eleven years,\" she said. \"Professional surveys with robotic telescopes find thousands now, but they cannot watch everything, and amateurs still make genuine discoveries. Most nights you will find nothing, and that is the job. If you need to find something in order to feel useful, this work will make you miserable.\"\n\nShe gave him a long list of galaxies to check, each one a pair of images, along with a notebook in which to record what he observed. For the first hour he clicked through them quickly, the way he skimmed reading assignments for English class, and he finished well before she expected. She looked at his notes, then picked three galaxies at random and made him check them again while she watched. In the second one, she pointed to a faint dot near the edge of the frame that he had completely missed.\n\nHe asked whether it was a supernova, suddenly alert, but she shook her head. \"It's an ordinary star in our own galaxy that happens to sit in front of that one, and it appears in both images,\" she said. \"But you did not see it at all, which means you were not really looking; you were only clicking.\"\n\nHe felt his face grow warm, and the fact that she did not seem angry somehow made it worse.\n\nBy the time he drove home after midnight, he had checked sixty-one galaxies and found nothing in any of them. The desert stretched black and enormous on either side of the road, and through the windshield he could see more stars than he had ever noticed from town. He thought about Margit sitting alone in that small dome for twenty-two years, waiting patiently for a dot to blink, and he could not decide whether that was the most boring thing he had ever heard of or something else entirely, something for which he did not yet have a word.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why did Tomás choose to volunteer at the observatory?","options":["He needed service hours and wanted to avoid trash duty","He had studied supernovae for several years at school","His physics teacher required every student to go there"],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Margit's remark that they are 'hunting' reveal about how she sees the work?","options":["It is a fast contest that she always expects to win","It is a hobby she no longer takes very seriously","It is a patient search, not a show of beautiful images"],"answer":2},{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Margit do after looking at Tomás's first notes?","options":["She praised him for working so quickly","She made him check three galaxies again","She sent him home early for the night"],"answer":1,"evidence":"She looked at his notes, then picked three galaxies at random and made him check them again while she watched."},{"type":"inference","q":"How does Tomás feel about the work by the end of the chapter?","options":["Unsure whether it is dull or meaningful","Certain that he will quit the next day","Proud that he found a new supernova"],"answer":0}],"grade":10,"words":804,"auto":{"fk":9.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-book-ch2","unit":"G10-U2","pool":"book","title":"A Dot That Was New","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Patient Sky","chapter":2,"text":"By October, Tomás had checked more than two thousand galaxies, and he had stopped clicking. He had learned to divide each image into quarters and examine them one at a time, the way Margit did, and to compare the brightness of familiar stars in order to judge whether the night had been clear enough to trust. He still found nothing, but he had begun to find the nothing interesting, because each galaxy was a city of billions of stars and he was one of perhaps a handful of people on Earth looking at it that night.\n\nHe was nearly at the end of a long list on a Tuesday evening when a galaxy in the constellation Pisces flickered on the screen, and something blinked.\n\nHe stopped breathing and flipped the images again. In the old photograph, the galaxy's spiral arm was smooth and continuous, but in the new one, just off the bright center, sat a small, sharp point of light that had not been there before.\n\n\"Margit,\" he said, and his voice came out strange and thin, \"I think I have something.\"\n\nShe rolled her chair over without hurrying and studied the screen for a long time before she answered. \"Yes, you have something,\" she said finally, \"and now we find out what it is.\"\n\nTomás had imagined this moment very differently, with shouting, perhaps a phone call to a university, perhaps even his name in a headline. Instead Margit opened a document titled CHECKLIST and turned the laptop toward him.\n\n\"A new dot can be many things,\" she explained. \"It can be a supernova, but it can also be an asteroid that happened to drift across the field when we took the picture, or a cosmic ray, a particle from space that struck the camera and left a single bright pixel. It can be a variable star, one that brightens and fades on its own schedule, or simply a flaw in the camera. Our job tonight is to eliminate everything boring before we allow ourselves to believe in anything exciting.\"\n\nThey went down the list together. First she had the telescope take three new images of the same galaxy, several minutes apart. The dot appeared in all three, which ruled out a cosmic ray, since a particle would not strike the same spot again and again. Next she entered the position into a public database maintained by the Minor Planet Center, an organization that keeps track of asteroids and comets, and no known asteroid was listed at that location. Finally she searched catalogs of variable stars and recent discoveries, and those turned up nothing as well.\n\nTomás felt his excitement rising with every step, and he announced that the discovery must be real. \"It is still possible,\" she corrected him gently. \"An unknown asteroid would also pass every one of those tests tonight. Asteroids move, though, so if we photograph this galaxy again tomorrow and the dot has stayed in place, we will have a much stronger case.\"\n\nHe could hardly believe she wanted to wait, and he asked what would happen if someone else found it first. She only shrugged. \"Then someone else finds it first,\" she said. \"That star exploded long before either of us was born, and its light has been traveling toward us for millions of years, so it can wait one more day while we make sure we are not fooling ourselves.\"\n\nBut the next night, clouds rolled in from the west, thick and low, and the dome stayed closed; the night after that, the clouds remained. Tomás refreshed the weather forecast on his phone so often that his mother asked whether he was waiting to hear from someone special. He kept the image of the galaxy open on his laptop, staring at the dot, which glowed on the screen like a secret he had promised to keep.\n\nOn the third cloudy night he typed a message to Margit asking whether they could at least share the image online, just to get other astronomers looking. Her reply arrived within a minute and contained only four words: \"No. Wait for the sky.\"\n\nHe read it several times. Then he put the phone face down on his desk and stared at the ceiling, feeling as though the universe had handed him an extraordinary gift and then locked it in a box.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What did Margit do instead of celebrating the discovery?","options":["She called a nearby university","She opened a checklist document","She posted the image online"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Instead Margit opened a document titled CHECKLIST and turned the laptop toward him."},{"type":"literal","q":"Why did seeing the dot in three new images rule out a cosmic ray?","options":["A particle would not strike the same spot again","The camera had been cleaned earlier that night","Cosmic rays only appear on very cloudy nights"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The dot appeared in all three, which ruled out a cosmic ray, since a particle would not strike the same spot again and again."},{"type":"why","q":"Why does Margit insist on waiting another night?","options":["She wants to share credit with others","The telescope needs a day to cool down","Only movement would reveal an asteroid"],"answer":2},{"type":"craft","q":"What does the final image of a gift locked in a box suggest about Tomás?","options":["He has received a present from his mother","He feels a discovery is being kept from him","He believes the dot was never really there"],"answer":1}],"grade":10,"words":726,"auto":{"fk":8.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-book-ch3","unit":"G10-U2","pool":"book","title":"Eleven Thousand Shares","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Patient Sky","chapter":3,"text":"On the fourth night the clouds were still there, and Tomás finally broke.\n\nHe told himself he was not really announcing anything. He posted the two images side by side on an astronomy forum he had been reading for months, with an arrow pointing to the dot and a caption he rewrote six times before he was satisfied: \"Possible new supernova in a galaxy in Pisces, found at a small observatory in New Mexico, not yet confirmed.\" Then, because the forum was slow, he posted the same images on his own social media account, where he had four hundred followers, most of them classmates who had never shown any interest in astronomy.\n\nBy morning the post had been shared eleven thousand times. A popular science account with millions of followers had reposted it with the headline \"Teenager discovers exploding star,\" and the careful words about confirmation had disappeared somewhere along the way. Tomás's phone buzzed through first period and second period and lunch, and a reporter from a television station in Albuquerque sent him a message asking for an interview. His chemistry teacher congratulated him in front of the whole class, and Tomás smiled and said thank you while something cold settled in his stomach.\n\nThat night the sky cleared, and he drove to Ridge Road faster than he should have. Margit was already in the dome with the telescope pointed at the galaxy, and she did not mention the post, so he could not tell whether she had seen it.\n\nWhen the new image appeared on the screen, Tomás flipped it against the image from four nights earlier, and his heart dropped.\n\nThe dot had moved. It was no longer beside the galaxy's center but had drifted a short distance toward the edge of the spiral arm, a small, steady shift that could mean only one thing. It was an asteroid, a chunk of rock inside our own solar system, passing in front of a galaxy millions of light-years behind it, like a moth crossing a movie screen.\n\nNeither of them spoke for a while. \"It was faint and moving slowly, which happens when an asteroid is near a turning point in its path across our sky,\" Margit said at last. \"It was a difficult case, and many careful people would have been fooled for one night.\"\n\nTomás pointed out that careful people would not have been fooled for two, and she agreed quietly that they would not.\n\nHe took out his phone and saw that the comments on his post had changed overnight. Someone on the forum had checked older survey images and noticed the motion before he had, and a thread was already mocking \"the kid who discovered a rock,\" while another person had written that amateurs should leave real science to professionals. He deleted the post with shaking fingers, although he knew that deleting it would not undo anything.\n\n\"You can say it,\" he told Margit. \"I should have waited, and you told me to wait.\"\n\nShe was quiet for a moment. Then she pulled a battered notebook from a shelf above the desk and opened it to a page near the front, dated 1975, where a sketch of a fuzzy smudge beside a star had been drawn in faded blue ink above two words in capital letters: NEW COMET?\n\n\"I was twenty-three, and I called the newspaper in Albuquerque before I told anyone else,\" she said. \"They printed my announcement on page three. The following week a professional astronomer explained, very politely, in a letter to the same paper, that my comet was a reflection of a bright star inside my own telescope, and I did not look through an eyepiece again for almost a year.\"\n\nWhen he asked what had made her come back, she answered simply that she had missed it. \"I also realized that I had been embarrassed for the wrong reason,\" she said. \"I believed my mistake was being wrong, but everyone is wrong constantly. My real mistake was being in a hurry to be seen.\"\n\nShe closed the notebook and slid it back onto the shelf. \"You did nothing wicked, Tomás,\" she said. \"You wanted the world to look at something you found, and that is a very human wish. But the sky does not care how many people are watching; it only cares whether you checked.\"\n\nTomás nodded slowly. Through the open slit in the dome he could see a narrow strip of black sky, crowded with stars, none of them in any hurry at all.","questions":[{"type":"why","q":"Why did Tomás post the images before the dot was confirmed?","options":["Margit told him to share the images widely","A reporter asked him to post them online that night","He was impatient and wanted people to see his find"],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"What did the new image show when the sky cleared?","options":["The dot had grown brighter","The dot had moved","The dot had faded away"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The dot had moved."},{"type":"craft","q":"What does the comparison to 'a moth crossing a movie screen' suggest?","options":["A small, nearby object passed before a distant one","A huge object blocked the whole galaxy from view","An insect had landed on the telescope's main lens"],"answer":0},{"type":"theme","q":"What lesson does Margit draw from her own mistake in 1975?","options":["Amateurs should leave discoveries to experts","Newspapers should never print science claims","Hurrying to be seen is worse than being wrong"],"answer":2}],"grade":10,"words":759,"auto":{"fk":8.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g10-book-ch4","unit":"G10-U2","pool":"book","title":"Second Light","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Patient Sky","chapter":4,"text":"Winter came early to the high desert. Through November and December, Tomás drove to Ridge Road two or three nights a week, long after his community service hours were complete, and he did not tell his friends where he was going, partly because the jokes about rocks had not entirely stopped and partly because he no longer felt he needed anyone to know. He checked galaxies and wrote in his notebook. He found nothing, and then more nothing, and somewhere along the way he stopped counting.\n\nMargit began leaving him alone in the dome for an hour at a time while she went inside to make tea. The first time she did it, he recognized it as a kind of trust, and he worked more carefully than ever.\n\nIt was on one of those evenings, in the middle of January, that a galaxy in the constellation Ursa Major blinked.\n\nTomás felt the same jolt in his chest as before, the same rush of heat to his face, but this time he did not call out. He took a slow breath, opened the document titled CHECKLIST, and began at the top.\n\nHe had the telescope take three more images, and the dot appeared in all of them. He entered its position into the Minor Planet Center's database and found no known asteroids nearby, and he searched the catalogs of variable stars and recent discoveries and found nothing listed there either. He measured the dot's brightness against nearby stars and recorded the numbers in his notebook. By the time Margit returned with two steaming mugs, he had completed every step on the list except the last one, and the last one was simply time.\n\nShe looked at the screen and then at his notebook, reading each line carefully before she spoke. \"Well,\" she said, \"tomorrow, then.\" Tomás agreed, and to his own surprise, he meant it.\n\nThe next night was clear and bitterly cold, and the dot was still there. It had stayed perfectly still against the galaxy, and it was slightly brighter than the night before. Margit compared it against images from a professional survey that had covered the same patch of sky a week earlier, and in those images the dot was absent. Whatever it was had appeared within the last few days, and it was sitting in a distant galaxy rather than drifting through our solar system.\n\n\"Now,\" she said, \"we report it.\"\n\nShe showed him how to submit the discovery to the Transient Name Server, the official system that astronomers around the world use to register new objects in the sky. Each report required precise coordinates, the time of the observation, the brightness, and the names of the discoverers. Tomás filled in the fields slowly, double-checking every number, but when he reached the last field, he hesitated and asked whose name should go first.\n\nMargit said that he had found it. Tomás replied that she had taught him how to know he had found it, and for a moment she simply looked at him. Then she smiled, and it was the first time he had ever seen her look genuinely surprised. \"Put them both,\" she said. \"The order hardly matters, because what matters is that it's true.\"\n\nThe report received an official designation within minutes, a string of numbers and letters beginning with the year. Four days later, a team using a large telescope in Chile took a spectrum of the object, splitting its light into a rainbow of colors to reveal what it was made of. The pattern was unmistakable: it was a Type Ia supernova, the explosion of a white dwarf star, so bright that for a few weeks it would outshine billions of ordinary stars in its galaxy.\n\nThere was no television interview this time, and Tomás posted nothing except a short line on the astronomy forum with a link to the official report. A few people replied with congratulations, and one of them was the user who had first noticed his asteroid's motion in October. \"Nice work,\" the message said. \"Properly done.\"\n\nOn his last night before spring break, Tomás stood outside the dome while the telescope worked, his breath clouding in the cold. Somewhere above him, in a galaxy he could not see with his eyes, light from the explosion was still arriving, light that had left its star long before there were people on Earth to notice it. He thought about how long that light had traveled to land on one small camera on Ridge Road, and about how little he had been willing to wait the first time.\n\nBehind him the door creaked, and Margit handed him a mug of tea. Neither of them said anything, because they did not need to; the sky was clear, and there were galaxies still to check.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Tomás do right after he saw the galaxy blink?","options":["He opened the checklist and began at the top","He called Margit into the dome","He posted the image on the astronomy forum"],"answer":0,"evidence":"He took a slow breath, opened the document titled CHECKLIST, and began at the top."},{"type":"inference","q":"How has Tomás changed since the third chapter?","options":["He now wants to become a television reporter","He now refuses to share any discovery at all","He now values careful checking over attention"],"answer":2},{"type":"inference","q":"Why is the forum user's message 'Properly done' meaningful to Tomás?","options":["It comes from the reporter who once contacted him","It comes from someone who saw his first mistake","It comes from his chemistry teacher at school"],"answer":1},{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the full story develop?","options":["Truth requires patience more than recognition","Professional scientists are wiser than amateurs","Luck matters more than skill in discovery"],"answer":0}],"grade":10,"words":803,"auto":{"fk":8.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}}];
